      <p class="meta"><time datetime="2026-09-02">Published September 2, 2026</time></p>

      <p>Six weeks ago I wrote about the <a href="/blog/hisense-a10-e-ink-phone-epub-2026/">Hisense A10</a> and made a specific claim about what set it apart. The A10's colour LCD snapped off the back magnetically; the Bigme HiBreak Dual 2's second screen was bolted in. That was the differentiator — the one hardware decision that made the A10 interesting rather than just another two-screen curiosity.</p>

      <p>That claim is now out of date. Bigme announced the HiBreak Dual 3 on August 29, 2026, and the headline change is precisely the thing I said Bigme did not do: the rear LCD is detachable. It attaches by magnets and metal pins that carry both data and power, and it pops off when you want a plain, distraction-free e-ink phone.</p>

      <p>I want to be honest about what this article can and cannot tell you, because Bigme has published remarkably little. There is no price. There is no release date. There is no screen size, no resolution, no battery capacity, and no statement about which E Ink panel generation is inside. I tried to pull the specifications from Bigme's own site and got a 114-byte shell — the page is client-rendered and returns nothing to a plain fetch. So everything below comes from Good e-Reader's announcement coverage, and where a number is missing I say so instead of guessing.</p>

      <h2>What Bigme actually confirmed</h2>

      <p>The front of the phone is a colour E Ink panel running at an 85 Hz refresh rate. That number is the one worth pausing on. Refresh rate is the single biggest complaint people have about reading and navigating on e-paper, and 85 Hz is far above what a traditional e-reader manages. It is the same design philosophy behind the <a href="/blog/bigme-b6-pro-color-ereader-2026/">Bigme B6 Pro</a>, which leans on a high refresh rate to feel like a phone rather than an e-reader.</p>

      <p>The E Ink panel also takes a stylus with 4096 levels of pressure sensitivity, which is a genuine specification rather than marketing language — it puts annotation and sketching on the same footing as a dedicated note-taking slate.</p>

      <p>Underneath, the phone runs a MediaTek Dimensity 8300. For an e-ink device that is an unusually capable chip; most e-paper hardware ships with something considerably weaker, and the gap is why so many e-ink phones feel sluggish in ways the screen alone does not explain. Bigme offers two memory configurations: 12 GB of RAM with 256 GB of storage, or 16 GB with 512 GB. There is 5G with dual-SIM support, and the phone runs Android 16 with full Google Play Store access.</p>

      <p>With the LCD module detached, the back shows a redesigned pill-shaped camera housing with a single lens and an LED flash. The overall footprint, per Bigme, matches the previous model's compact profile — though no millimetre or gram figures were given, so treat that as a comparative statement rather than a measurement.</p>

