      <p>Five of those rows are blank, and that is the story as much as the filled ones are. An announcement with no price, no date, and no panel generation is a positioning move — Bigme staking a claim to the detachable design before the Hisense A10's launch momentum settles.</p>

      <h2>How the detachable design happened twice in eight weeks</h2>

      <p>The chronology is tight enough to be worth laying out, because it explains why this announcement reads the way it does.</p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>July 10, 2026</strong> — Bigme's HiBreak Dual goes live on Kickstarter, billed as the first E Ink plus LCD dual-screen 5G phone with stylus support and Android 16.</li>
        <li><strong>July 11, 2026</strong> — Hisense officially reveals the A10. Its rear display is magnetic and detachable, and coverage frames that explicitly against Bigme's fixed second screen.</li>
        <li><strong>August 29, 2026</strong> — Hisense A10 and A10 Ultra go on sale. The same day, Bigme announces the HiBreak Dual 3 with a detachable LCD module.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>Seven weeks from "Bigme's screen is built in" to "Bigme's screen comes off." Whether that was already on the roadmap or a fast response, the practical result is the same: detachable is no longer a reason to pick one over the other. If you were waiting to see whether the A10's removable panel was a one-off experiment or the direction the whole category was heading, you have your answer.</p>

      <h2>What this means if you read EPUB files</h2>

      <p>Here is the part that gets lost in dual-screen coverage, and I want to be blunt about it: the detachable LCD is close to irrelevant for reading.</p>

      <p>When you read an EPUB, you read on the front E Ink panel. That is true of the A10, it is true of the Dual 2, and it is true of the Dual 3. The LCD exists for video, social feeds, and anything where e-paper's response time gets in the way. Snapping it off changes the weight in your hand and removes a distraction — both real benefits — but it does not change the reading surface, the text rendering, or how your library gets onto the device.</p>

      <p>What actually governs your reading experience on all three phones is Android 16 with Play Store access. That means:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>You install whatever reader app you prefer — <a href="/blog/koreader-zen-ui-epub-2026/">KOReader</a>, Moon+ Reader, Librera — rather than being locked to a vendor's built-in app.</li>
        <li>Your EPUB files transfer over USB, cloud storage, or a sync app, with no store or format gatekeeping in between.</li>
        <li>Library borrowing through Libby works as it does on any Android device.</li>
        <li>Sideloaded EPUB files behave identically whether the LCD module is attached or in a drawer.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>This is the same practical conclusion I reached with the <a href="/blog/boox-palma-epub-reading-2026/">Boox Palma</a>: on an Android e-ink device, the operating system is what determines whether your library is portable, and full Play Store access is worth more than any single hardware feature. It is the difference between a device that reads your files and a device that reads its store's files.</p>

      <p>One thing genuinely does matter for reading, and it is the 85 Hz front panel. Page turns, scrolling through a long chapter, and moving around a table of contents are where low refresh rates hurt. Whether 85 Hz translates into a noticeably better reading feel is something no spec sheet can answer — but it is the specification most likely to affect your day-to-day use, far more than the module on the back.</p>

      <h2>Should you wait for it?</h2>

      <p>You cannot buy it, so the question is really whether to hold off on something else.</p>

      <p>If you want a two-screen e-ink phone today, the Hisense A10 and A10 Ultra have been on sale since August 29, with published specifications and a known price. The Dual 3 has neither. Waiting on an unpriced, undated announcement means waiting an unknown length of time for a device whose panel generation has not even been disclosed.</p>

      <p>If you are shopping for reading rather than for a phone, I would look elsewhere entirely. A dedicated e-reader or a pocketable e-ink device like the Palma will read EPUB files more comfortably, cost less, and last longer on a charge than a 5G phone carrying a detachable LCD. The dual-screen category is interesting engineering; it is not the most efficient way to read a book.</p>

      <p>There is also a pricing backdrop worth keeping in view. Memory prices have been climbing hard through 2026, and as I covered in the <a href="/blog/amazon-memory-crisis-60-percent-device-price-hike-2026/">device price hikes driven by the memory crunch</a>, RAM and storage are exactly what is getting more expensive. A phone offered in 12 GB and 16 GB configurations is heavily exposed to that. Bigme's silence on price may not be purely strategic.</p>

      <p>My read: the Dual 3 is a signal, not a purchase. It tells you the detachable design won, and that within one product cycle it stopped being anyone's exclusive advantage. If you have been putting off a dual-screen phone because you were not sure the format would stick, this is reasonable evidence that it will. Just do not confuse that with a device you can plan around yet.</p>

